.TH "lazdebugtargetstarter" "1" "12 August 2026" "Lazarus" "Lazarus Debug Target Starter"
.SH "NAME"
lazdebugtargetstarter \- The Lazarus helper to start a debug target in a terminal.

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B lazdebugtargetstarter
.I <pipename> <program> [arguments]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.B lazdebugtargetstarter
is used by the
.B Lazarus
IDE to run a debugged program in a real terminal, so that the program can use
the terminal for its own input and output while the debugger (gdb or FpDebug)
controls it.

The IDE creates a named pipe and starts a terminal, which runs this tool with
the name of that pipe and the command line of the program to debug. The tool
creates a second named pipe, reports its own process ID and the name of that
second pipe to the IDE, and then waits until the IDE answers, that is until the
debugger has attached to the process. Finally the tool replaces itself with the
program to debug (exec), so the debugged program keeps the process ID the
debugger attached to and runs in the terminal.

If the IDE does not answer within two minutes, or if any pipe cannot be created
or opened, the tool removes its pipe and exits without starting the program.

The named pipes are created in
.B /tmp
(or in the current directory, if that fails) with names starting with
.BR laz_fpdebug_starter_p_ .

This tool is only useful on Linux and is normally not called by the user.

.SH "OPTIONS"
No options. The first argument is the name of the pipe created by the IDE, the
second argument is the program to run, all further arguments are passed to that
program. If called with less than two arguments it does nothing.

